## Proof

**Claim.** Let $M$ be an $m$-dimensional smooth manifold, $p \in M$, and $v \in T_p M$. Then there exists a smooth curve $\gamma:(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)\to M$ with $\gamma(0)=p$ and $\dot\gamma(0)=v$.

**Setup.** Take the standard definition of $T_pM$ as the vector space of derivations at $p$: linear maps $v:C^\infty(M)\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfying the Leibniz rule
$$v(fg)=v(f)\,g(p)+f(p)\,v(g).$$
Equivalently, in any chart $(U,\varphi)=(U,x^1,\dots,x^m)$ about $p$, every $v\in T_pM$ has a unique coordinate expansion
$$v=\sum_{i=1}^{m} v^i\,\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial x^i}\right|_{p},\qquad v^i=v(x^i)\in\mathbb{R}.$$

**Construction of the curve.** Fix such a chart with $\varphi(p)=a=(a^1,\dots,a^m)\in\mathbb{R}^m$, and write $v=\sum_i v^i\,\partial_{x^i}|_p$. Define, for the coordinate vector $w=(v^1,\dots,v^m)\in\mathbb{R}^m$,
$$\gamma(t)\;=\;\varphi^{-1}\!\bigl(a+t\,w\bigr),\qquad t\in(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon),$$
where $\varepsilon>0$ is chosen small enough that $a+tw$ stays inside the open set $\varphi(U)\subseteq\mathbb{R}^m$ for all $|t|<\varepsilon$ (possible since $\varphi(U)$ is open).

**Smoothness.** The map $t\mapsto a+tw$ is smooth $\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}^m$, and $\varphi^{-1}:\varphi(U)\to U$ is smooth by definition of a smooth chart. Hence $\gamma$ is smooth as a composition of smooth maps.

**Passes through $p$.** $\gamma(0)=\varphi^{-1}(a)=\varphi^{-1}(\varphi(p))=p$.

**Derivative equals $v$.** For any $f\in C^\infty(M)$, the velocity $\dot\gamma(0)$ acts as a derivation by
$$\dot\gamma(0)(f)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0} f(\gamma(t))=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}(f\circ\varphi^{-1})(a+tw).$$
By the chain rule in $\mathbb{R}^m$,
$$\dot\gamma(0)(f)=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\frac{\partial(f\circ\varphi^{-1})}{\partial x^i}\Big|_{a}\,w^i=\sum_{i=1}^{m}v^i\,\frac{\partial(f\circ\varphi^{-1})}{\partial x^i}\Big|_{\varphi(p)}=\sum_{i=1}^{m}v^i\,\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial x^i}\right|_{p}(f)=v(f).$$
Since this holds for every $f\in C^\infty(M)$, we conclude $\dot\gamma(0)=v$ as derivations.

**Chart-independence.** The curve $\gamma$ was built using one chart, but the conclusion $\dot\gamma(0)=v$ is a statement about the derivation $\dot\gamma(0)$, which is chart-independent. So the result does not depend on the choice of chart; any chart witnessing the coordinate form of $v$ produces a valid curve.

**Conclusion.** Every tangent vector $v\in T_pM$ is realized as the velocity $\dot\gamma(0)$ of some smooth curve $\gamma$ in $M$ with $\gamma(0)=p$. The answer to the question is **yes**.

